  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2025 CD4046 DRC Acute Crisis Joint Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The intervention will target a total of 17100 children, families &amp; community members in Nyragongo &amp; Kiroshe. It will build on and expand WC&apos;s response in both locations via the CM. WC, KUA &amp; MIDEFEHOPS will address key needs &amp; gaps through 4 core activities that will ensure effective &amp; urgent CP interventions, as well as MPC distribution to affected HHs. 1) 120 children will be reunified with their families. Where necessary, UASC will be placed with transitional host/foster families who will receive all necessary resources. We will engage with local actors &amp; ACJR partners to ensure joined up referral mechanisms are in place for cases, &amp; support DIVAS to ensure swift reunification of children with placements in foster families where necessary.  2) 6 CFSs providing safe areas for 7200 children and caregivers to engage in various activities including group &amp; individual PSS and play-based learning. CFS placement will be coordinated with other humanitarian actors to make the most of opportunities when &amp; where communities are gathering for other activities incl. e.g. food distribution. 3) 17100 community members will receive awareness raising &amp; 6 committees will be established for risk analysis, reduction plans &amp; mitigation measures to reduce protection risks 4) MPC for 1500 identified individuals (300 HHs). WC implemented mobile cash distribution in recent projects &amp; have established working relationships with 2 key mobile networks, &amp; our bank in Goma (both of which are confirmed as operational again) to ensure swift &amp; secure fund transfers. Phones are provided if households do not have access &amp; transfers are followed up with PDM. CVWG standards will be followed. We will collaborate with CARE and our common local partner KUA in Kiroshe, and with Help A Child in Nyiragongo. We will intervene in separate districts to avoid overlap, and coordinate establishment of a joined-up referral mechanisms to ensure collaborative and streamlined case management. &quot;</narrative>
  </description>
